I made 3D mode a bit easier and gave it a bigger cube; 2D and 2.5D work exactly as before. I haven't playtested it. I could only edit files here, so nothing was run in a browser.

**Bigger playspace** (`index.html`)
- The 3D cube goes from 10×10×10 to **14×14×14**. That's 2.7× the volume, and each layer now has 196 cells, about half of the 400 on the flat 20×20 board. I stopped at 14 because a 20-cell cube gets hard to read in the wireframe view.
- I moved the default camera back (distance 20 → 28) so the whole cube still fits on screen, and widened the scroll-zoom limits from 13–34 to 18–46.
- The layer gauge on the right side of the 3D view grows to fit the 14 layers.
- The start-screen hint now says "14×14×14 CUBE".

**Slightly easier, 3D only**

| | 2D / 2.5D | 3D before | 3D now |
|---|---|---|---|
| Starting speed (ms per move) | 150 | 150 | **175** |
| Speed-up per food | −4 ms | −4 ms | **−3 ms** |
| Top speed (ms per move) | 55 | 55 | **75** |

The 3D settings sit in their own constants next to the existing ones, so they're easy to adjust. Wall and self collisions, the arrow-key steering (which follows the camera angle), and the rise/dive keys all read the cube size, so they work with the larger cube without other changes.

When you try it, check that the cube fits the screen at the default zoom and that 3D feels less rushed. If it now feels too slow or too empty, lower `BASE_TICK_3D` or `SIZE3` (a 12-cell cube would sit in between).
